# 6. Typography Structure

The typography system should follow a consistent hierarchy.

Recommended categories include:

- Display
- Headline
- Title
- Body
- Label
- Caption

These categories describe typography roles, not necessarily the exact names of existing CSS tokens.

The implementation must use the actual typography tokens discovered in `design-tokens.css`.

## 6.1 Display

Used for:

- Homepage hero headline
- Major introductory statements
- Prominent editorial messages
- Large featured quotations

Typeface: Cormorant Garamond.

Display text should feel expressive, confident, spacious, and memorable.

## 6.2 Headline

Used for:

- Main section headings
- Page introductions
- Major content headings
- Featured editorial statements

Typeface: Primarily Cormorant Garamond.

Headline typography should create strong separation between major content sections.

## 6.3 Title

Used for:

- Subsection headings
- Featured content titles
- Writing titles
- Grouped content headings
- Supporting editorial headings

Typeface: Primarily Cormorant Garamond, with DM Sans where a more functional or compact treatment is appropriate.

## 6.4 Body

Used for:

- Biography paragraphs
- Professional experience descriptions
- Service and advisory descriptions
- Speaking information
- Article summaries
- Long-form editorial content
- Supporting explanations

Typeface: DM Sans.

Body typography must prioritise readability, comfortable line spacing, and clear paragraph structure.

## 6.5 Label

Used for:

- Navigation items
- Buttons
- Metadata
- Categories
- Small interface descriptions
- Form labels
- Section identifiers

Typeface: DM Sans.

Labels should remain compact, legible, and consistent.

## 6.6 Caption

Used for:

- Image descriptions
- Photography captions
- Supporting notes
- Publication metadata
- Dates and contextual information

Typeface: DM Sans.

Caption typography should remain readable and visually subordinate to the main content.

