# 8. Type Scale and Sizing Rules

Typography must follow a consistent scale.

The exact font sizes must be derived from the existing typography tokens in `design-tokens.css`.

Do not invent arbitrary font-size values or create a separate scale without first inspecting the existing design system.

### Recommended hierarchy

- Display: Largest and most expressive typography.
- Headline: Major section and page headings.
- Title: Subsection and content headings.
- Body: Main reading text.
- Label: Navigation and functional interface text.
- Caption: Supporting metadata and secondary information.

### Type Scale Principles

- Maintain proportional relationships between hierarchy levels.
- Ensure headings are clearly distinguishable from body text.
- Avoid unnecessarily large supporting headings.
- Avoid excessively small body text.
- Preserve a consistent scale across sections.
- Use responsive scaling where appropriate.

Typography should establish a predictable visual rhythm throughout the website.

---

# 9. Font Weight Rules

Font weight should reinforce hierarchy without creating unnecessary visual complexity.

Use the available font weights defined by the existing project and loaded font files.

### General principles

- Display headings should use an appropriate weight that preserves the elegance of Cormorant Garamond.
- Major headings should have sufficient visual presence.
- Body text should remain comfortable to read.
- Navigation and labels should remain clear.
- Bold weight should be reserved for meaningful emphasis.
- Avoid using too many different weights within one section.

Do not assume that every font weight is available.

Verify the actual font files and existing font declarations before referencing specific weights.

---

# 10. Line Height and Letter Spacing

Line-height and letter spacing must support readability and visual balance.

### Display Typography

- Use a controlled line-height that allows large headings to feel cohesive.
- Avoid excessive spacing between lines.
- Adjust line breaks to create intentional compositions.
- Use letter spacing carefully, particularly with large serif headings.

### Body Typography

- Use comfortable line-height for paragraphs.
- Maintain sufficient separation between paragraphs.
- Avoid compressed body text.
- Ensure long-form content remains comfortable to read.

### Navigation and Labels

- Maintain consistent line-height.
- Use restrained letter spacing.
- Avoid excessive tracking that makes functional labels difficult to scan.

### Implementation

Use the existing line-height and letter-spacing tokens in `design-tokens.css`.

Do not hardcode inconsistent values across individual components.

---

# 11. Readability and Content Rhythm

Typography must support extended reading sessions and comfortable content scanning.

Requirements:

- Maintain readable line lengths.
- Avoid dense text blocks.
- Use consistent paragraph spacing.
- Create clear heading-to-paragraph relationships.
- Maintain sufficient spacing between content groups.
- Use appropriate line-height.
- Avoid unnecessary uppercase text.
- Ensure captions and metadata remain legible.
- Maintain sufficient contrast against all backgrounds.

### Long-Form Content

The About Me and Writing sections may contain longer editorial content.

For these sections:

- Avoid excessively wide paragraphs.
- Use meaningful subheadings where appropriate.
- Break long content into digestible sections.
- Maintain a consistent reading rhythm.
- Avoid unnecessary text decoration.
- Keep supporting metadata visually subordinate.

Typography should make the content feel approachable rather than overwhelming.

---

# 12. Responsive Typography

Typography must adapt appropriately across:

- Desktop
- Tablet
- Mobile

The website is expected to provide a refined experience across different screen sizes.

### Desktop

- Use display typography confidently.
- Preserve strong headline hierarchy.
- Allow sufficient whitespace around major headings.
- Maintain comfortable paragraph widths.
- Avoid excessively long text lines.

### Tablet

- Adjust display sizes and line breaks where necessary.
- Preserve heading hierarchy.
- Maintain balanced content proportions.
- Prevent text from becoming crowded.

### Mobile

- Scale display typography appropriately.
- Prevent oversized headlines from dominating the viewport.
- Avoid awkward single-word line breaks.
- Maintain comfortable body text sizing.
- Preserve readable paragraph widths.
- Ensure navigation and buttons remain legible.
- Avoid horizontal overflow.

### Responsive Scaling

Use CSS `clamp()` where appropriate for fluid typography.

However:

- Base typography values on existing design tokens.
- Inspect the current responsive token structure first.
- Do not introduce arbitrary responsive values.
- Ensure text scaling remains controlled across breakpoints.
- Test actual rendered layouts rather than relying only on calculated values.

Typography must feel intentional on mobile, not like a reduced desktop layout.

